  "review": {
    "book": "2CH",
    "entityID": "person:abijah_38",
    "primaryWitnesses": [
      {
        "ref": "2CH 11:20",
        "textualBasisJa": "After her, he took Maacah the granddaughter of Absalom; and she bore him Abijah, Attai, Ziza, and Shelomith."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 11:22",
        "textualBasisJa": "Rehoboam appointed Abijah the son of Maacah to be chief, the prince among his brothers, for he intended to make him king."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 12:16",
        "textualBasisJa": "Rehoboam slept with his fathers, and was buried in David’s city; and Abijah his son reigned in his place."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:1",
        "textualBasisJa": "In the eighteenth year of King Jeroboam, Abijah began to reign over Judah."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:2",
        "textualBasisJa": "He reigned three years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name was Micaiah the daughter of Uriel of Gibeah. There was war between Abijah and Jeroboam."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:3",
        "textualBasisJa": "Abijah joined battle with an army of valiant men of war, even four hundred thousand chosen men; and Jeroboam set the battle in array against him with eight hundred thousand chosen men, who were mighty men of valor."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:4",
        "textualBasisJa": "Abijah stood up on Mount Zemaraim, which is in the hill country of Ephraim, and said, “Hear me, Jeroboam and all Israel:"
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:15",
        "textualBasisJa": "Then the men of Judah gave a shout. As the men of Judah shouted, God struck Jeroboam and all Israel before Abijah and Judah."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:17",
        "textualBasisJa": "Abijah and his people killed them with a great slaughter, so five hundred thousand chosen men of Israel fell down slain."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:19",
        "textualBasisJa": "Abijah pursued Jeroboam, and took cities from him: Bethel with its villages, Jeshanah with its villages, and Ephron with its villages."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:20",
        "textualBasisJa": "Jeroboam didn’t recover strength again in the days of Abijah. The LORD struck him, and he died."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:21",
        "textualBasisJa": "But Abijah grew mighty and took for himself fourteen wives, and became the father of twenty-two sons and sixteen daughters."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 13:22",
        "textualBasisJa": "The rest of the acts of Abijah, his ways, and his sayings are written in the commentary of the prophet Iddo."
      },
      {
        "ref": "2CH 14:1",
        "textualBasisJa": "So Abijah slept with his fathers, and they buried him in David’s city; and Asa his son reigned in his place. In his days, the land was quiet ten years."
      }
    ],
